<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	  The relying-party association (spec 019 T138).

	  Without this entry the passkey provider refuses every ceremony for
	  getvela.app, so the app cannot create OR use a wallet — the whole feature
	  is inert. The file was empty until this feature checked it, which is
	  exactly the kind of gap that only shows up on a device.

	  The other half already exists and was verified live: the
	  apple-app-site-association file on the production domain returns 200 as
	  application/json and lists this app under `webcredentials`:

	      {"webcredentials":{"apps":["F9W689P9NE.app.getvela.VelaWallet", …]}}

	  Both halves must name the same domain. `webcredentials:` is what passkeys
	  read; `applinks:` is the separate association the /sign deep link uses, and
	  it is listed in the same AASA file.
	-->
	<key>com.apple.developer.associated-domains</key>
	<array>
		<string>webcredentials:getvela.app</string>
		<string>applinks:getvela.app</string>
	</array>

	<!--
	  Smart-card access, for the app-owned CTAP2-over-CCID path (spec 019 T171b).

	  CryptoTokenKit's `TKSmartCard` is the ONLY way a third-party iOS app can
	  talk to an external security key over USB — iOS exposes no USB-HID host
	  API. That path consults NO domain association: it is the escape hatch a
	  lapsed or down association cannot padlock (FR-009c).

	  THIS KEY IS NOT WHAT MAKES IT WORK. `com.apple.security.smartcard` is a
	  macOS entitlement. On iOS 16 and later `TKSmartCardSlotManager.default`
	  is always available — the iOS SDK's `TKSmartCard.h` says so in words
	  ("iOS: The defaultManager instance is always accessible"; Apple DTS,
	  developer forums 119030 and 735215) — no iOS provisioning profile can
	  carry the key, and Xcode drops it at signing: it is absent from the
	  archive's .xcent and from the App Store export's entitlements (spec 088,
	  2026-10-01). Measured 2026-10-09 on an iPhone 11 (iOS 26.5.2): a
	  development-signed build with no such key has a non-nil slot manager;
	  and the route was device-verified end to end on 2026-08-28 (iPhone 15
	  Pro, YubiKey 5C, firmware 5.8) in a build that lacked it too. So every
	  signed build — Debug on a device, TestFlight, App Store — runs the USB
	  route exactly as this file's presence or absence leaves it: unchanged.

	  What the route does need is physical: a USB-C port and a key that offers
	  FIDO over its smart-card (CCID) interface — a YubiKey on firmware 5.8 or
	  later. A Lightning key, an NFC key and an older key go through Apple's
	  own security-key sheet (`SmartCardCtapCeremony.SystemSheetFallback`).

	  Kept only as a harmless marker of intent; deleting it changes nothing.
	-->
	<key>com.apple.security.smartcard</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
